# Raw footage and renders. These are large, regenerable, and belong on the
# machine that has the camera -- not in the history.
videos/
projects/
previews/
broll/out/
broll/public/video/
broll/public/transcripts/
broll/public/overlay-assets/
broll/public/slides/
broll/props.generated.json
broll/props.verify.json

# Carousels: the words are committed, the pictures are not. A `slides.json`
# is the tool -- it names the format, the headline and where the crop sits --
# but the photograph it points at is the operator's own material, and the
# stills under out/ are whatever `python pipeline/slides.py` last made of
# them. So the repository carries every definition and no picture, and a
# clone renders a sample once its own photographs are dropped in beside it.
#
# Two levels deep, not one: a story can live at stories/<set>/<slide>/, and
# `stories/*/out/` matched only the shallow case.
stories/**/out/
stories/**/*.jpg
stories/**/*.jpeg
stories/**/*.png
stories/**/*.webp
stories/**/*.mp4

# Reference material -- other people's reels, screenshots and tutorial
# transcripts. Studied on the machine that downloaded them and never
# redistributed from here. What was learned from them is written up in the
# .md files beside them, and those ARE committed: the findings are ours, the
# footage is not.
howtocutvideo/videoreferences/
howtocutvideo/imagereferences/
howtocutvideo/backgroundreferences/
howtocutvideo/claude-editing-tutorial.txt
howtocutvideo/usingtranscript.txt

# The idea stage's inputs are the operator's business, not the tool. The
# questionnaire ships as `BRAND.template.md`; the answered `BRAND.md` -- what
# the business is, who buys, what a post actually did -- stays on the
# operator's machine, and so does whatever raw material they feed the stage.
# What is *learned* from that material goes in `pipeline/IDEAS.md` under
# Learned rules, and that is committed. The folder is the evidence; IDEAS.md
# is the finding.
pipeline/ideas/BRAND.md
pipeline/ideas/sources/*
!pipeline/ideas/sources/README.md

# A backstop under all of the above, by extension rather than by folder.
# Every rule so far names a place, and the place is what gets forgotten:
# `videos/` was ignored from the first commit and the operator's photographs
# reached a public repository through `stories/`, which nobody had thought of
# yet. A folder invented next week is covered by this and by nothing else.
#
# Footage, audio and transcripts only. Images are left to the rules above on
# purpose -- a diagram or a committed catalogue render is legitimately a .png,
# and a blanket rule here would quietly swallow the documentation.
*.mp4
*.mov
*.m4v
*.mkv
*.avi
*.heic
*.wav
*.m4a
*.mp3
# A transcript is the words out of the operator's mouth, so it is as private
# as the recording, wherever it is written. No negation for the two fixtures
# already tracked: .gitignore does not apply to a file git already follows,
# and re-admitting their folder would re-admit every real transcript
# `render_captions` copies into it.
*.words.json

.venv-check/
node_modules/
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
*.pyc
